#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282998 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3003d2c6-3b38-5e95-b719-5b9a0d5c9642" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283000} {:answer/id #uuid "02ed77c0-f3e4-541a-8b4b-30cb5e3a1ed7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283001} {:answer/id #uuid "dab393c9-b76c-5660-bb79-30c2d41d6439" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283002} {:answer/id #uuid "e84b7c88-92b9-57ba-a71c-0fc867044a59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283003} {:answer/id #uuid "58eefba0-9a07-54df-9842-45ae83596040" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283004}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02ed77c0-f3e4-541a-8b4b-30cb5e3a1ed7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283001} :answer-field/id #uuid "746eb990-d795-58ac-b486-d547ad6ba17a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282999}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a9233943-9639-5785-9a46-de416a7119f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-15641" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=9-\\frac{2}{3}x$ and the point $P(3,7)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$  calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(7).$" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(3,7).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =9-\\frac{2}{3}x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(3,7)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(7,3)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(7) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(7))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283012 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function Using a Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d35ad3ef-28f1-557d-bbec-6ef6c4696fc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283014}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d35ad3ef-28f1-557d-bbec-6ef6c4696fc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283014} :answer-field/id #uuid "071430b0-ac1c-5c5d-bfb0-7aacd0d96541" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283013}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ec34106c-1275-5721-882b-cb32e2aa950e" :question/math-academy-id "q-342589" :question/problem "Find $({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1)$ given the following table of values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $-1$ | $6$ |\n| $4$ | $3$ | $-2$ |\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-1))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(-1)=-2$ and ${f}^{′}(-2)=6.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
